('Premise 1 · the Ratzinger citation as footnote 1; facets; Evangelium Vitae',47,
 'St. John Paul II traced the same line, and named its cost, a decade earlier:',
 'St. John Paul II traced the same line a decade earlier: "when the sense of God is lost, the sense of man is also threatened and poisoned" (*Evangelium Vitae* 22). And he named its cost:'),
('Premise 1 · the Evangelium Vitae blockquote, §20 alone',50,
 '"When the sense of God is lost, the sense of man is also threatened… any reference to common values and to a truth absolutely binding on everyone is lost, and social life ventures onto the shifting sands of complete relativism. Everything is negotiable,',
 '"In this way, any reference to common values and to a truth absolutely binding on everyone is lost, and social life ventures on to the shifting sands of complete relativism. At that point, everything is negotiable,'),
('Natural law · "all people in all circumstances"; an idea can never love anyone back',119,
 'but they cannot ground that wrong in anything beyond their own opinions—and an opinion, however strong, cannot make a wrong objective.',
 'but, as we have just seen, they cannot ground that wrong in anything with the authority to bind all people in all circumstances. A standard with no one behind it can be enforced, and it can even win devotion, but it can never love anyone back; communion happens only between persons, and the Truth that draws us is a Person (cf. John 14:6).'),
('Natural law · the Church\'s words, and why she holds this hope',121,
 'The Church\'s answer is careful and honest. The four pillars of love are not',
 'The Church\'s answer is careful and honest. All salvation comes from Christ: "the one Christ is the mediator and the way of salvation; he is present to us in his body which is the Church" (CCC 846). Truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice are not'),
('Natural law · the Church\'s words, and why she holds this hope',121,
 'This follows the same logic as the sacraments: God binds *us* to the sacraments as the ordinary means of grace — but God is not Himself bound by them. He can pour grace through any channel He chooses. Those who follow the light they have been given — the natural law, the grace active in conscience, the partial truth written on their hearts — are, in a real sense, doing the will of the Father, even without yet knowing the Father\'s name. God\'s perfect justice gives each person what they are due, accounting for what was offered to them and not what was withheld through no fault of their own (CCC 1257, 1260; *Lumen Gentium* 16). But none of this cancels the necessity of the Church',
 '"God has bound salvation to the sacrament of Baptism, but he himself is not bound by his sacraments" (CCC 1257). And so the Church teaches that "those who, through no fault of their own, do not know the Gospel of Christ or his Church, but who nevertheless seek God with a sincere heart, and, moved by grace, try in their actions to do his will as they know it through the dictates of their conscience — those too may achieve eternal salvation" (CCC 847, quoting *Lumen Gentium* 16). Such people are, in a real sense, doing the will of the Father, even without yet knowing the Father\'s name, and whatever grace reaches them is Christ\'s grace. Why does the Church hold this hope? Because "since Christ died for all, and since all men are in fact called to one and the same destiny, which is divine, we must hold that the Holy Spirit offers to all the possibility of being made partakers, in a way known to God, of the Paschal mystery" (CCC 1260). Every person bears the same God-given dignity; God\'s justice does not hold against anyone what he could not know; and His mercy can be at work in the love a person pours out for God, neighbor and self before he has the words to say why. That love does not save by itself; it is grace already at work. The Council is just as honest that "often men, deceived by the Evil One, have become vain in their reasonings" (*Lumen Gentium* 16), and the Church "still has the obligation and also the sacred right to evangelize all men" (CCC 848). None of this cancels the necessity of the Church'),
('Integration · and the reverse: what breaks from God\'s design breaks down',125,
 'The way love integrates truth, justice, mercy, sacrifice, covenant, life-giving, and communion into one coherent whole points to intelligent design. These facets could conflict—truth could oppose mercy, justice could contradict love—but in God\'s perfect love, they integrate perfectly.',
 'The way love holds together what is sacred, covenant, truth, justice, mercy, sacrifice and life-giving communion as one coherent whole points to intelligence. These facets could conflict—truth could oppose mercy, justice could crush it—but in God\'s perfect love they are not parts kept in balance; they are one. And the reverse is just as telling: every philosophy or ideology that tears one facet away from the others, truth without mercy or mercy without truth, breaks down when it is lived, as Chapters 6–9, on truth, justice, mercy and sacrifice, showed one facet at a time.'),
('Personal encounter · not every unbeliever is culpable; the materialist argues with what is not matter',131,
 'Yet some still don\'t believe—not because the evidence is insufficient, but because "they suppress the truth" (Romans 1:18). Atheism requires us to believe that objective moral truth emerged from mindless matter, that universal obligations arose from evolutionary accidents.',
 'Yet some still don\'t believe—not because the evidence is insufficient. Sometimes the reason is a wound or an ignorance that is no fault of their own; sometimes it is what St. Paul warns of, holding down a truth we would rather not face (cf. Romans 1:18). Atheism, in its usual materialist form, asks us to believe that objective moral truth emerged from mindless matter and that universal obligations arose from evolutionary accidents. Yet notice how it makes its case: it appeals to our reason, asks us to weigh the evidence and to follow the truth, and none of those is made of matter. The materialist must use what is not material to convince us that nothing is real but matter.'),
('Law of the Gospel · St. Paul\'s "law of Christ" first',145,
 'The Catholic Church gives us a beautiful name for this law: **the Law of the Gospel** (CCC 1965-1974). This is not a burden imposed from outside, but God\'s own interior law of love written on our hearts through the Holy Spirit.',
 'And in Christ, God brings that law to its fullness. St. Paul calls it the law of Christ: "Bear one another\'s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Christ" (Galatians 6:2). The Church calls it **the New Law**, or **the Law of the Gospel**: "the perfection here on earth of the divine law, natural and revealed" (CCC 1965). It is not a burden imposed from outside, but the interior law of love that the Holy Spirit writes on the hearts of those who believe (CCC 1965-1966).'),
